A||N||11||Patrick86GT||88 rack bushing replacement query||||1||
Z||000000||Patrick86GT||07-07-2001||09:13 PM||||I've removed all the stuff required to get to the "plastic" passenger side rack bushing on my 88GT. I have my Dickman replacement in hand ready to install but....I am not seeing a plastic bushing in the end of the rack housing; it's steel, w/ 3 slots in it. Attempting to use a sharp 1/4" wood chisel to cut the "plastic tabs" in the slots, I found nothing to cut. And this steel "bushing" is a wee bit rusted into the housing, so I juiced it heavily w/ penetrant.  Yet, it won't budge: won't go in, won't come out, won't even rotate.   <P>Any insights would be greatly appreciated!!!<P>PS: Today my goal was to install a new AC compressor and the rack bushing on the 88GT. The &$%@* compressor took 5.5 hours.  Another 2.5 has been spent on the rack w/ major frustration in getting the old bushing out!  Sometimes, I hate my fiero collection....until I drive one, and it's love all over again!||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||07-07-2001||09:32 PM||||To get the plastic bushing out I take a flat screwdriver and pry a tab down. I insert an flat electical connector to hold the tab down while I work on the next one. After the third tab being held down I can pull the bushing out easily. <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||07-07-2001||11:28 PM||||The 88 racks originally had a steel rack bushing that was changed to the plastic type late in the 88 model run.  It is held by a retaining ring that needs to be pried out before the bushing can be removed.  <P>When I had my Formula, it had the metal rack bushing.  I found the only way I could remove the bushing was to completely remove and disassemble the rack internals, then use a long pipe inserted into the rack housing from the drivers side to hammer the bushing out of the housing.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick86GT||07-08-2001||12:02 AM||||Ah-Ha!  Thanks guys!  I think Bob has shed some light on my problem....mine must be an early 88.  Tomorrow, the entire rack gets removed and the long pipe diven as suggested!<BR>What's another 3-4 hours?  <P>Fiero Laws: <BR>1) If you can see the part to be worked on, you can't see it and work it concurrently.<BR>2) If you can't see the part to be worked on, feel for it. <BR>3) If you can feel it w/ you finger(s), no wrench fits into the available space except the shortest handled rachet with which you can't get enough leverage to break it loose anyway.<BR>4) Air rachets rarely help.<BR>5) Don't let anyone other than a fellow Fiero Fanatic work on your car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick86GT||07-08-2001||12:13 AM||||Skitime!  That's a great lookin' T-top!  I recently bought one (88 dark red) for my "keeper."  It needs minor work that I will begin after I sell my red 86GT & the wife's black 88GT  <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/prs46530/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/prs46530/index.html</A> <P>right now, i'm fixing the odds n ends on my wife's car that I kinda let be low on the list last winter....oh, did i mention, she expects to drive the t-top?   huh||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||07-08-2001||12:20 AM||||Anyone have an aproximate production # stating where the "plastic-bushing-production" began?<P>I wonder if they decided a stronger bushing wouldn't be needed later in production since p/s was cancelled? Just a thought.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||07-08-2001||01:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Patrick86GT:<BR><B>Skitime!  That's a great lookin' T-top!  I recently bought one (88 dark red) for my "keeper."  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Hey......I just got a burgundy '88 GT T-Top too!  It has every available option, including the lumbar seat.  This brings me to my next question......as much as I'd love to have another black '88 GT (I've owned 4!) I can't afford it.....would you be interested in selling the lumbar seat?  Mine doesn;t work (bladders are shot).  I could trade you a very nice regular cloth GT seat + $$.  LMK.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||rodmcneill||07-08-2001||01:33 AM||||I will have to replace the pass. side bush. in my 88 also. This was news to me that some were steel and others plastic, I thought they were all plastic, thats why they wore out. I have not checked yet but mine is probably steel(early 88 model). I was planning on getting Rodney's bushing but it is made out of aluminum, won't this wear out even faster than the original steel bushing? I see in the Fiero Store catalog there rebuilt racks use bronze bushings with grease fittings. Does anyone know if these bronze bush. are available separately. It sounds like this job is a pain in the a$$ and I would like to do it one time only.||||reg||1
